    "updated_at": "2025-04-19T17:59:04Z",
    "body": "> Perhaps it needs to be browsing-context connected?\n\nI don't think we want to restrict it to browsing-context connected, because all browsers follow the spec right now in the detached document case, in which nodes are \"connected\", even though none are browsing-context connected. See:\n\n```js\nconst iframe = document.createElement('iframe');\ndocument.body.append(iframe);\nconst innerDoc = iframe.contentDocument;\nconst innerDiv = innerDoc.body.appendChild(innerDoc.createElement('div'));\niframe.remove();\n\nconst r1 = innerDoc.createElement('input');\nr1.type = 'radio';\nr1.name = 'group';\nr1.checked = 'true';\nconst r2 = innerDoc.createElement('input');\nr2.type = 'radio';\nr2.name = 'group';\nr2.checked = 'true';\ninnerDiv.append(r1, r2);\n\nconsole.log(innerDiv.children[0].checked); // false\nconsole.log(innerDiv.children[1].checked); // true\n```\n\nSo these steps need to be activated whenever nodes are connected, not just browsing-context connected. I think the real issue  is with the `innerHTML` setter. Browsers don't actually consider nodes appended to the intermediate document it creates as \"connected\", while the spec apparently does. So a perfect reading of the spec would lead you to consider the two input radios nodes were indeed \"connected\" at some point briefly, and change the WPT expectations to match the spec and fail in all browsers.\n\nBut since all browsers do the same thing that deviates from the spec, we should consider changing the spec to match browsers. I can imagine two ways of doing this:\n - Change the definition of \"connected\" to somehow exclude nodes in this intermediate \"parser document\"\n - Change the parser that `innerHTML` creates, to not be a normal/full \"Document\" that \"connected\" would apply to; this'd leave \"connected\" alone, and make it organically false in these contexts\n\nI guess it'd be good to hear from implementers to see how they all seem to align on `innerHTML = ` never creating connected nodes. @mfreed7 do you have any insight from Chromium's perspective?",
